>> title: Tonkotsu Ramen
>> category: Noodles
>> tags: pork, japanese, ramen, noodles, assembly
>> counters: Ramen Shop
>> aka: tonkotsu ramen, tonkotsu, 豚骨ラーメン, とんこつラーメン, Hakata ramen, pork bone ramen, white ramen, a bowl of tonkotsu
>> pairs-with: tonkotsu-broth, shio-tare, mayu, ramen-noodles, chashu
>> servings: 1
>> time: 10 min
>> step.1: Assembly, not cooking. Everything is already hot, the bowl goes together in ninety seconds, and it is eaten immediately.
>> step.2: tare and mayu into a scalded bowl
>> step.3: boiling broth in, stir once
>> step.4: boil 15 sec, drain hard
>> step.5: noodles in, straightened with chopsticks
>> step.6: lay the toppings, serve at once

Assembly, not cooking. Everything is already hot, the bowl goes together in ninety seconds, and it is eaten immediately. Scald the bowl with boiling water first and tip it out — a cold bowl takes ten degrees off the soup before it reaches the table.

Ladle @shio tare{1 1/2%Tbs}(22 mL) and @mayu{1%tsp}(5 mL) into a scalded #ramen bowl{}.

Pour @tonkotsu broth{1 1/2%cups}(350 mL; at a full boil) into @&(~1)tare and oil{} and stir once, no more. Taste it now: this is the only moment the seasoning can still be fixed.

Boil @ramen noodles{5%oz}(140 g; 1.5 mm straight) in @water{4%qt}(3.8 L; hard-boiling, unsalted) ~boil{15%sec}, then lift them out and shake the basket hard three or four times. Fifteen seconds is barikata, the Hakata default; water carried into the bowl on the noodles is what makes a shop bowl taste watery, so the shaking is not fussiness.

Lift @&(~1)drained noodles{} into @&(~2)seasoned broth{} and straighten them into one direction with chopsticks so they lie flat rather than knotting.

Lay @chāshū{3%slices}(warmed), @kikurage{2%Tbs}(soaked and shredded), @beni shoga{1%Tbs}(red pickled ginger), @scallion{1%stalk}(thinly sliced) and @toasted sesame seeds{1%tsp} over @&(~1)bowl{} and take it to the table. Thin noodles in a hot bowl keep about three minutes.
